{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Eat, my son, honey, for it is good, and dripping honey upon your palate — so know wisdom for your soul; if you find it, there is a future, and your hope will not be cut off (Proverbs 24:13–14).</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, likened wisdom — which is an intellectual pleasure — to honey, which is a bodily pleasure. And so he consistently does in most of his words in the book of Proverbs: he speaks by means of parables in order to warn a person to bring his intellect to bear upon his nature. For man is compounded of two things, intellect and nature, and it is impossible for him to bring his intellect to bear upon his nature except by means of a parable drawn from things perceived, in which nature is immersed. Know that the ranks of the lower creations are four, one above the other: the inanimate (domem), the vegetative (tzome'ach), the animal (chai), and the rational (medabber). The inanimate — mountains and hills — has no soul at all: neither vegetative soul nor locomotive soul, let alone rational soul. Above it is the vegetative, which has an advantage over the inanimate because it possesses a vegetative soul. Above the vegetative are the rest of the animals, which have a locomotive soul and a vegetative soul. Above the non-speaking animals is man, who includes all of them in the vegetative soul and locomotive soul within him, and who has an advantage over all of them in the rational soul. And so you find the order of their creation in the account of Genesis: the inanimate is what is written (Genesis 1:9) ״ותראה היבשה״; the vegetative is what is written after it, ״תדשא הארץ״; the animal is what is written on the fifth day, ״ישרצו המים שרץ נפש חיה״; the rational is what is written on the sixth day, ״נעשה אדם בצלמנו״. For among the lower creations you find that the last is the most excellent — the opposite of the upper creations, where the first is the most excellent: the light on the first day, the firmament on the second day (which is below the light), and the luminaries on the fourth (which are below the firmament)." "<b>And since</b> man shares with the vegetative in the vegetative soul and with the beast in the locomotive soul, he therefore needs a rational soul to make him wise and to teach him the way of the intellect, so that his intellect may prevail over his nature. From this you learn: just as the vegetative is exalted above the inanimate, so the animal is exalted above the vegetative, and the rational above the animal. And even though this is clear and perceptible to the eyes, the verses will still instruct us in this — as it is written (Job 35:11): ״מלפנו מבהמות ארץ ומעוף השמים יחכמנו״. And together with this advantage, which is the intellect, one tests the measure of the intellect and the measure of nature, each in its respective rank. And when a man makes his intellect prevail over his nature — behold, he is an angel of YHWH of Hosts; and when he makes his nature prevail over his intellect, he is likened to ״כבהמות נדמו״. And of this David the king, peace be upon him, warns a person to follow the intellect, as he said (Psalms 32:8): ״אשכילך ואורך בדרך זו תלך איעצה עליך עיני״, and he further warns us not to follow nature, as he said after it (ibid.): ״אל תהיו כסוס כפרד אין הבין במתג ורסן עדיו לבלום בל קרוב אליך״." "<b>Now behold,</b> Solomon informed us in the book of Proverbs of the benefits of the intellect and the harms of nature. For nature is lazy about seeking food, drink, clothing, and shelter — which are necessary activities — while the intellect rebukes it to be diligent in what is necessary and shows it the outcome of things: that with food one is saved from hunger, with clothing one is saved and covered so as not to stand naked, and with shelter one is saved from cold and heat. For the intellect knows and discerns all this, whereas nature knows nothing of it. And of this Solomon said (Proverbs 12:27): ״לא יחרוך רמיה צידו והון אדם יקר חרוץ״ — its meaning: a deceitful man does not retain his wealth in his hand. And since the way of a hunter is to singe the wings of birds so they will not escape, therefore he says that one who steals and roasts his quarry will not succeed in singeing, for the bird will escape him before he can singe it — and this is a parable for one who amasses wealth unjustly, for he will not succeed. And יחרוך is from (Daniel 3:27): ״ושער ראשיהן לא התחרך״. But the precious wealth that abides in a person's hand is the trait of diligence. Nature seeks robbery, theft, and fraud, and these actions are pleasing to it to perform — the reason being that it feels within itself the need and the pressure it has. But the intellect arouses and rebukes it in this, and shows it the consequence: that through this will come shame, blemish, and disgrace in this world, and severe punishment in the World to Come. And of this Solomon said (Proverbs 20:17): ״ערב לאיש לחם שקר ואחר ימלא פיהו חצץ״. Nature seeks joy, laughter, and intercourse — the reason for this being the matter of desire planted in it for the perpetuation of the species, and it finds sweetness when it satisfies its desire. But the intellect rebukes us and shows it the consequence: that the sweetness will turn into bitterness. And of this Solomon said (ibid. 5): ״כי נופת תטופנה שפתי זרה וחלק משמן חכה ואחריתה מרה כלענה חדה כחרב פיות״. He mentioned חרב פיות (a two-edged sword) because a person cannot escape the harm — like a sword that has two edges, so that one is injured by this side or by that side. So too one who follows after nature cannot escape the harm of nature: he will be punished either in this world or in the World to Come. And since every creature with sensation — and likewise beasts and wild animals — when it sees fire flees from it out of fear lest it be burned, and also flees from deep waters out of fear lest it drown, and if it hears a frightening sound it flees and hides out of fear — and all this is in nature — therefore the intellect informs nature: just so must you guard yourself; if you see an evil deed, flee from it; or if you hear something improper, flee and go away. And it brings proof from his own conduct and nature, for behold, he flees from the burning fire, from the pit, and from the deep river. Therefore Solomon always admonishes regarding matters of the intellect and draws a parable for them from natural parables. Trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, he likens to a strong tower — as he said (Proverbs 18:10): ״מגדל עוז שם ה׳ בו ירוץ צדיק ונשגב״ — because in the way of nature, those who fear an enemy find refuge in a strong tower, and there they consider themselves safe. And just as nature knows this and sees it always, so should one know that one who trusts in His Name, blessed be He, sits secure as one who sits in a tower — and more so, for ״בו ירוץ״, that is, in the Name of YHWH the runner runs and is exalted from trouble, which is not the case with a tower, for there one is not always safe from trouble, since it is possible that the enemies will besiege the tower and those sitting there are not delivered from their affliction. The heart of a king in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, he likened to streams of water — as he said (ibid. 21): ״פלגי מים לב מלך ביד ה׳ וגו׳״. For the sin of robbery he brought the parable of birds caught in a net through desiring the grain they see within it — and that desire brings them to death when they are caught in the net; so too this robber: the desire for wealth brings him to death. And therefore Solomon the king, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 1): ״כי חנם מזורה הרשת בעיני כל בעל כנף והם לדמם יארובו יצפנו לנפשותם כן ארחות כל בוצע בצע את נפש בעליו יקח״. And for the sin of harlotry he brought the parable of falling into a deep pit — as he said (ibid. 23): ״כי שוחה עמוקה זונה ובאר צרה נכריה״, and it is written (ibid. 22): ״שוחה עמוקה פי זרות זעום ה׳ יפל שם״. And the sin of one who reveals a secret he likened to a city that has no wall — as he said (ibid. 25): ״עיר פרוצה אין חומה איש אשר אין מעצור לרוחו״. And the sin of a married woman he likened to walking on coals, from which it is impossible not to be burned — and this is what he said (ibid. 6): ״היחתה איש אש בחיקו ובגדיו לא תשרפנה אם יהלך איש על הגחלים ורגליו לא תכוינה כן הבא אל אשת רעהו לא ינקה כל הנוגע בה״. He stated the parable in two ways: carrying fire in one's bosom is a parable for merely touching one of her limbs — for even though his body is not burned, his garments are burned; and walking on coals is a parable for actual intercourse — this is what he said: ״כן הבא אל אשת רעהו לא ינקה כל הנוגע בה״, that is, from the judgment of Gehenna. And we learn explicitly that it is impossible for him to escape punishment: either through the burning of his garments for mere touch, or through the burning of his body in Gehenna for intercourse. And all this is in order to bring nature toward the side of the intellect, for they are far apart and there is a great difference between them — like the difference between light and darkness. As the verse says (Ecclesiastes 2:13): ״וראיתי אני שיש יתרון לחכמה מן הסכלות כיתרון האור מן החושך״. Nature discerns only what is before it; it does not contemplate or attend to the end of things — for one who sows wheat, how deficient he appears in the eyes of nature, for he buries it in the ground and destroys it, and nature would rather eat it than soil it and bury its splendor and beauty. But the intellect disciplines us and teaches us the outcome of knowledge: that through this one will produce another like himself, and from one measure many measures will be added — for otherwise the grain would be cut off and all creatures would die. And a proof from Scripture that the intellect contemplates the end of things is what is written (Deuteronomy 32:29): ״לו חכמו ישכילו זאת יבינו לאחריתם״." "<b>And to</b> this intention the Torah elaborates in the accounts of the generations, to teach about the fate (aharit) of the righteous who followed the intellect and the fate of the wicked who followed nature — as in the account of the generation of the Flood, who were destroyed by the Flood because they were wicked, and the salvation of Noah and his sons from the Flood because they were righteous. For the intellect is what attends to and understands the end of all things. Nature chooses to speak lies and slander; it loves ease and laziness; it despises charity out of fear lest it become impoverished; it despises fasting lest it become ill; and it despises wisdom so that it need not toil and labor in it. But the intellect warns us to refrain from ease and laziness — and of this Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 10:18): ״בעצלתים ימך המקרה ובשפלות ידים ידלוף הבית״, and it is written (Proverbs 20:4): ״מחורף עצל לא יחרוש ושאל בקציר ואין״. It refrains from speaking lies, as is said (ibid. 6): ״יפיח כזבים עד שקר״, and David the king, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 101:7): ״דובר שקרים לא יכון לנגד עיני״. It refrains from slander — as he said (Proverbs 18:21): ״מות וחיים ביד לשון ואוהביה יאכל פריה״. He commands one to strive in the trait of charity — as he said (ibid. 11): ״וצדקה תציל ממות״, and it is written (ibid.): ״יש מפזר ונוסף עוד״, and He informed us that on account of giving charity his wealth will not lack — as he said (ibid. 28): ״נותן לרש אין מחסור״. He commands one to strive in learning wisdom, for it is the intellect that recognizes its worth, not nature; and He revealed to us that with wisdom one acquires reward in one's end in this world and in the end of one's soul in the World to Come. One's end in this world is what Solomon said (ibid. 19): ״שמע עצה וקבל מוסר למען תחכם באחריתך״; the end of one's soul in the World to Come is what Solomon said (ibid. 24): ״כן דעה חכמה לנפשך אם מצאת ויש אחרית״. Solomon taught us that the wisdom a person knows belongs to one's soul — for he did not say דעה לך חכמה but said דעה חכמה לנפשך, meaning that it is for your soul; אם מצאת — if you have found it, meaning if you have attained it; ויש אחרית ותקותך לא תכרת — he explained here that the recompense and reward is eternal, never to be lost. And the two verses are tied together thus: just as eating honey is good for the body and sweet to the palate, so too דעה חכמה — knowledge of wisdom — in which you strive and which you will find sweet, and that benefit shall be for your soul. And the reason he likened wisdom to honey rather than to other delicacies is that the sense of taste perceives sweetness, and honey is compatible with the temperament of the liver, for the liver is hot and moist and honey too is hot and moist. Therefore the desire planted in the liver is aroused toward it more than toward anything else, and a person craves it more than all delicacies, because the temperament of honey in its sweetness suits the temperament of the liver. And therefore Solomon likened wisdom to honey and said: just as your body perceives the sweetness of honey, so too is your soul obligated to perceive the sweetness of wisdom. And therefore he said כן דעה חכמה לנפשך — for the benefit of honey is only for the body, while the benefit of wisdom is for the soul. אם מצאת החכמה ויש אחרית — wisdom benefits you in this world and in the World to Come, unlike honey, whose benefit and use are only in this world. ותקותך לא תכרת — that wisdom abides forever, unlike honey and other delicacies, which are cut off and cease; but wisdom never ceases — and this is ותקותך לא תכרת. And the reason he likened wisdom to honey is that among us there is no food sweeter than honey; yet wisdom is sweeter still, as his father David said (Psalms 19:11): ״ומתוקים מדבש ונופת צופים״. But it is in our hand to draw a parable only from perceptible things found among us, and the intention is to say: just as the body takes pleasure in the taste of honey, so the soul takes pleasure in the delight of wisdom. And so we find at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 24:11): ״ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו״ — he likened the pleasure of beholding the face of the Shekhinah, which is spiritual eating, to the pleasure of eating and drinking. And so too Moses' standing on the mountain for forty days without eating or drinking — for his soul took pleasure in the delight by which his body was sustained, like a person who eats and drinks and whose soul is sustained by the nourishment of his body. And this distinction Moses merited for one hundred twenty days, from the sixth of Sivan until the Day of Atonement, on which he brought down the second tablets. How so? On the sixth of Sivan the Torah was given; on the next day, the seventh, he ascended the mountain and stayed there forty days, which concluded on the seventeenth of Tammuz, on which he descended at six hours into the day and broke the tablets. On the next day, the eighteenth, he burned the calf and judged those who had served it. On the nineteenth he ascended the mountain and stayed there a second forty days to pray concerning that sin, and they concluded on the first of Elul, and he was told ״ועלית בבקר אל הר סיני״, and he stayed there a third forty days to receive the second tablets, and they concluded on the Day of Atonement — and I have already mentioned this in the portion of Ki Tissa. On that very day he brought down the tablets, and it was a day of pardon and atonement. When he ascended on Rosh Chodesh Elul it was a Monday, and when he descended on the Day of Atonement it was a Thursday. Therefore our Sages established it as a day of judgment, for courts are convene and sit on Mondays and Thursdays — and this is (Isaiah 55:6): ״דרשו ה׳ בהמצאו״ — it is fitting to seek on Monday and Thursday, so that He may be found, just as Moses did. On the day after the Day of Atonement he was commanded concerning the Mishkan — and that is the הקהל (assembly) mentioned here. And this is what is written:" "<b>And Moses assembled all the congregation of the children of Israel and said to them: These are the things which HaShem commanded to do them.</b> If you examine these portions you will find that the making of the Mishkan and all its vessels is mentioned many times. The Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned it to Moses twice: first He mentioned it in detail in the portion ויקחו לי, and He went back and mentioned it in general terms in כי תשא in the section ראה קראתי בשם. Third, it is that Moses mentioned it now to all Israel in general terms and commanded them concerning it, saying וכל חכם לב בכם יבאו ויעשו את כל אשר צוה ה׳ את המשכן את אהלו. Fourth, is the Torah's narrative after the fact, as it is written ויעשו כל חכם לב בעושי המלאכה, and it mentions concerning each and every item ויעש ויעש. Fifth, after all the work was completed, in the portion (Exodus 39:33) ויביאו את המשכן אל משה. And the matter of mentioning it five times was all for the preeminence of the Mishkan and by way of endearment, just as we find that Scripture mentions Israel five times in a single verse in the portion בהעלותך, as it is written (Numbers 8:19) ואתנה את הלוים נתנים לאהרן ולבניו מתוך בני ישראל לעבוד את עבודת בני ישראל באהל מועד ולכפר על בני ישראל ולא יהיה בבני ישראל נגף בגשת בני ישראל אל הקדש — and all by way of endearment and preeminence. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, compared it: to what is this like? To a king who sent his son to school. When he returned he asked: 'Did my son eat? Did my son drink? Is my son well? Did my son go to school? Did my son come back from school?' And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said concerning the Torah's lengthening in the narrative of Eliezer, where all his words are doubled: 'The mundane talk of the servants of the fathers before the Holy One, blessed be He, is finer than the Torah of the sons — for the portion of Eliezer, all its words are doubled, and it is spread over two or three pages, while many commandments in the Torah depend on a single letter or a single word, and they are the very bodies of the Torah.'" "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'Moses assembled' — let the assembly of Moses come and atone for the assembly of Aaron, as it is written (Exodus 32:1) ויקהל העם על אהרן. Let the saying of Moses come, as it is written ויאמר אליהם משה, and atone for the saying of Aaron, as it is said (ibid.) ויאמרו אליו קום עשה לנו אלהים. Let 'these things' come and atone for 'these are your gods, O Israel.' Let the gold of the wave-offering come and atone for the gold of the calf." "<b>These are the things which HaShem commanded to do them.</b> And adjacent to it: ששת ימים תעשה מלאכה — this comes to teach you that these things, the labors of the Mishkan, HaShem commanded to do them during the six days and not on the day of Shabbat, for the labor of the Mishkan does not override Shabbat. And what it says, תעשה מלאכה, and it did not say תעשה לנוכח — this is to teach that at a time when Israel do the will of the Omnipresent, their labor is done through others, and at a time when they do not do His will, you do it yourself; and not only that, but the labor of others is performed through them. And likewise in the sense of תעשה, you do it even for others."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:08:18.289493958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}